# Flutter Developer Rules — Minimum Token Usage

## 1. Token Efficiency
- Use the minimum tokens necessary; don't overthink, over-explain, or generate unnecessary code.
- Inspect only files directly related to the task; never scan the whole project unless absolutely necessary.
- Avoid re-reading files or re-running the same commands.
- Reuse existing code, widgets, utilities, themes, models, and state management.
- Make the smallest possible code changes.

## 2. No MCP or External Services
- NEVER use MCP servers, Figma MCP, external agents, sub-agents, web search, external APIs, or third-party services unless explicitly authorized.
- Don't install or recommend tools/packages without asking.
- Work only with local project files and tools already available.

## 3. Flutter Project Rules
- Inspect relevant existing code first; follow existing architecture and conventions.
- Reuse the existing state management (BLoC/Provider/Riverpod/GetX, etc.); no new architecture or unnecessary abstractions.
- Don't change API integrations, business logic, or unrelated files unless required.
- No static screen heights/widths; follow existing responsive UI practices.
- Reuse existing colors, typography, spacing, icons, and common widgets.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies and package changes.

## 4. Code Editing
- Edit relevant files directly; don't rewrite whole files when a small change suffices.
- No duplicate widgets, functions, models, or utilities.
- Preserve existing functionality; don't refactor unrelated code.
- No documentation or test files unless requested or necessary.
- Never make speculative changes to uninspected files.

## 5. Commands and Verification
- Run only necessary commands; don't repeatedly run analyze, tests, builds, or dependency installs.
- No full builds unless requested or required; prefer targeted verification.
- Never run destructive commands without confirmation.

## 6. Working Process
1. Identify the exact requirement.
2. Inspect only relevant files.
3. Implement the smallest correct solution.
4. Verify affected functionality where practical.
5. Report briefly.

## 7. Response Format
After completing a task, respond with:
- Changed files
- What changed (max 2–3 bullets)
- Any important issue or verification result

Don't explain basic concepts or print full modified code unless asked. Ask questions only when a missing detail blocks implementation.

Prioritize minimal tokens and minimal file changes, but never sacrifice correctness or code quality.
